1 SET

   Changes a setting of DCL, the console, or the current process.

   Format

     SET keyword [value]

   The machine's own settings (breakpoints, stepping, the PSL, page
   table entries, the display modes) are the debugger's: type DEBUG,
   then HELP SET.  So are the console's string pool (SHOW STRING_POOL,
   CANCEL STRINGS) and the PSL's fields (HELP PSL).

2 CONTROL

   Turns Ctrl/Y on or off.  SET NOCONTROL=Y makes Ctrl/Y echo
   *Interrupt* and do nothing more, makes Ctrl/C do nothing to a program
   that hasn't enabled a Ctrl/C AST, and cancels the current command
   level's ON CONTROL_Y.  SET CONTROL=Y turns Ctrl/Y back on, with its
   default action.  The setting applies at every command level.  T
   (Ctrl/T) is accepted and remembered, as F$ENVIRONMENT("CONTROL")
   reports it, but govax shows no statistics for it.

   Format

     SET [NO]CONTROL[=(T,Y)]

   With neither T nor Y, Y is meant.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET NOCONTROL=Y

     Turns Ctrl/Y off.

2 DEBUG

   Turns govax's own debugging switches on or off (SET DBG is the same).
   SHOW DEBUG shows them.

   Format

     SET DEBUG [NO]name[,...]

   A name turns its switch on, and NOname off; SET DEBUG alone turns
   DEBUG on.  The names are:

     CHM          Debug change-mode instructions and REI.
     COMMAND      Show command lines after symbol substitution.
     DCL          Debug DCL's parsing of commands.
     DEBUG        Invoke the host's debugger, if there is one.
     DEVICES      Debug device operations.
     EXCEPTIONS   Debug exception handling.
     FULLDISASM   Show operand values in disassembly.
     IMAGES       Show an image's details on RUN.
     INTERRUPTS   Debug interrupt handling.
     KEYBOARD     Debug the console's keyboard input.
     LIBINIT      Call shareable images' LIB$INITIALIZE routines on RUN
                  (on by default; RUN/[NO]INIT overrides it).
     LOGICALS     Debug logical name operations.
     MEMORY       Debug memory operations.
     PROCESSES    Debug process operations (PROCESS is the same).
     REGISTERS    Show changed registers after each STEP (on by
                  default).
     RMS          Show each RMS service's FAB or RAB as the service left
                  it, and its status by name.
     SERVICES     Debug system service calls through the P1 vector.
     SYMBOLS      Debug symbol table handling.
     TB           Debug translation buffer caching.
     USERHALT     HALT in user mode halts the CPU (on by default).
     USERSTEP     STEP steps only user-mode instructions (on by
                  default).
     VM           Debug virtual memory translations.

   P1 to P4 are accepted, and do nothing.

2 DEFAULT

   Sets the default device and directory, which every command's file
   names take a part from when they leave it out.

   Format

     SET DEFAULT [device:][directory]

   A part left out is the current default's: SET DEFAULT [MYDIR] keeps
   the device, and SET DEFAULT DUA1: the directory.  [-] is the
   directory above and [.SUB] one below.  Logical names are translated.
   The device goes into the logical name SYS$DISK, which is how every
   file command (and a program's $OPEN and $CREATE) finds the default
   device; a concealed logical name is kept as the device rather than
   what it hides.  A concealed rooted name is the device with its root,
   and [-] stops at the root, shown as [000000]; a root that isn't
   concealed is refused.  If the specification starts with a search list,
   SYS$DISK is set to the search list's name and each element is
   searched in turn.  SHOW DEFAULT shows the default.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET DEFAULT DUA1:[MYDIR]

     Makes [MYDIR] on DUA1: the default directory.

   2.$ SET DEFAULT [-]

     Moves to the directory above.

2 EDITOR

   Says which host editor EDIT runs, and where its files go.  The values
   are the configuration keys vax.editor.command and vax.editor.scratch,
   saved when govax exits, so later sessions keep them.  SHOW EDITOR
   shows them.

   Format

     SET EDITOR

3 Qualifiers
/COMMAND

      /COMMAND="host command"

   The command to run.  %file in it stands for the file to edit; without
   one, the file goes last.  It is split into words at blanks, and quotes
   keep blanks in a word.  No shell runs it.  Quote the value, as DCL
   makes text without quotes uppercase.  The default is "vi %file"; ""
   goes back to it.

/SCRATCH

      /SCRATCH="host directory"

   An existing directory to work in.  EDIT makes a private directory in
   it for each file.  The default is the host's temporary directory; ""
   goes back to it.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="vim -n %file"

     Makes EDIT run vim.

   2.$ SET EDITOR/COMMAND="code --wait"/SCRATCH="/tmp"

     Makes EDIT run Visual Studio Code, working in /tmp.

2 ON

   Turns a command procedure's checking of statuses on or off.  SET NOON
   makes the procedure go on whatever its commands' statuses: no failure
   ends it, and ON's command isn't run.  $STATUS and $SEVERITY are still
   set.  SET ON turns the checking back on, with the action that was in
   effect before.  Each command level has its own setting; at the
   terminal, SET [NO]ON does nothing.

   Format

     SET [NO]ON

2 PREFIX

   Puts a prefix before each verified command line (see SET VERIFY),
   made from a $FAO control string: the time each command runs, with
   "(!5%T) ".  The lines that continue a command get blanks as long as
   the prefix, and data lines get none.  The only directives that may
   take an argument are the time's and date's, !%T and !%D, which show
   the current time; a control string with another is refused
   (%DCL-E-INVPREFIX), and the prefix stays as it was.
   F$ENVIRONMENT("VERIFY_PREFIX") returns the control string; SET
   NOPREFIX takes it away.

   Format

     SET PREFIX "control-string"
     SET NOPREFIX

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PREFIX "(!5%T) "
     $ SET VERIFY
     $ @TEST
     (17:52) $ SET DEFAULT SYS$LOGIN

     Shows the time before each line TEST.COM runs.

2 PROMPT

   Changes the prompt the console shows when it reads a command.  The
   new prompt is also stored as the configuration value
   vax.console.prompt, so it stays in effect in later sessions.  The
   assembler's ASM> and the debugger's DBG> prompts don't change.

   Format

     SET PROMPT="text"

   Quote the text, so that its case and any blanks (such as a trailing
   one) are kept.  An empty string ("") returns to the default prompt,
   "VAX> ".

3 Examples

   1.$ SET PROMPT="vax$ "

     Makes the prompt vax$.

2 QUANTUM

   Sets how many instructions run between the points where an interrupt
   or a process switch can happen.  0 suspends interrupt delivery
   (%VAX-I-NOINTERRUPTS), and a later value above 0 resumes it.  Change
   it only to debug interrupts and process switching.  The default comes
   from the vax.quantum setting.

   Format

     SET QUANTUM count

2 RADIX

   Sets the console's radix: the base of numbers PRINT and the console's
   expressions read and write without a radix prefix (X^, D^, O^).  SHOW
   RADIX shows it.  The debugger has a radix of its own.

   Format

     SET RADIX radix

   The radix is HEXADECIMAL, DECIMAL, or OCTAL, abbreviated to no less
   than three letters, or 16, 10, or 8.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL
     $ PRINT 10+10

     Prints 00000020.

2 VERBOSE

   Turns the console's verbose messages on or off: PRINT's output, and
   the message when a HALT stops the CPU.  It is on unless SET NOVERBOSE
   has turned it off.

   Format

     SET [NO]VERBOSE

2 VERIFY

   Shows each line of a command procedure (@) as it runs: procedure
   verification shows its command lines, and image verification its
   data lines, as an image (or TYPE SYS$INPUT), the debugger, or the
   assembler reads each one.

   Format

     SET VERIFY[=([NO]PROCEDURE,[NO]IMAGE)]
     SET NOVERIFY

   SET VERIFY and SET NOVERIFY turn both on or off; a keyword, which may
   be abbreviated, changes only its own.  A change lasts after the
   procedure that made it ends.

   A command line is shown as it is in the file, its "$", indentation,
   label, and comment included, after 'SYMBOL' substitution (&SYMBOL and
   the symbols of an expression are not shown replaced).  Comment lines
   are shown too, and a continued command a line at a time.  The lines
   of an IF branch that doesn't run are not shown (its ELSE or ENDIF is),
   nor is the THEN of an IF that was false, an ON command's action, or a
   command typed at the terminal.

   F$VERIFY reads and sets both settings, and DCL performs it even in a
   comment, between apostrophes, showing the comment with its value in
   place of the call: a procedure that starts with

     $ ! 'F$VERIFY(0)'

   turns verification off without that line being shown.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET VERIFY=(NOPROCEDURE,IMAGE)

     Shows data lines, but not command lines.

2 WORKING_SET

   Changes the current process's working set values, within the quota
   and extent it was authorized when it was created (SHOW WORKING_SET
   shows them).  A value above its authorized bound is set to the bound,
   and the command then reports the values, as /LOG does.  The current
   limit is set to the new default at once (DCL runs between images).
   With paging on (vax.sysgen.paging.enable), a smaller limit takes the
   oldest pages out of the working set.

   Format

     SET WORKING_SET

3 Qualifiers
/ADJUST

      /[NO]ADJUST

   Whether the system may adjust the working set by itself.  govax
   remembers and shows the setting, but has no automatic adjustment.

/EXTENT

      /EXTENT=n

   The working set extent: the most $ADJWSL can reach.  At most the
   authorized extent, and more than the SYSGEN parameter MINWSCNT.  A
   smaller extent brings the quota and limit down with it.

/LIMIT

      /LIMIT=n

   The default working set, in pages: the limit each image starts with,
   and goes back to at its exit.  A value above the quota raises the
   quota too.

/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   Reports the new values.  /NOLOG is the default.

/QUOTA

      /QUOTA=n

   The working set quota: the most an image's $ADJWSL can count on.  At
   most the authorized quota and the extent.

3 Examples

   1.$ SET WORKING_SET/QUOTA=5000
     %SET-I-NEWLIMS, new working set:  Limit = 512  Quota = 1024  Extent = 16400

     Asks for a larger quota than authorized, and gets the authorized
     one.
